# The lockfile records the tarball URLs of whichever registry was configured
# when it was written. Left alone, `npm ci` follows those URLs literally -- so
# a workstation with a mirror configured and a CI runner with the public
# registry would need two different lockfiles, and the committed one would
# only ever work on the machine that produced it.
#
# `replace-registry-host=always` makes npm rewrite the host of every recorded
# URL to the registry the current environment is configured for, keeping the
# path. The public registry and the common mirrors use identical paths
# (`/pkg/-/pkg-<version>.tgz`), and the integrity hashes are identical too, so
# the swap is safe: the same lockfile installs from a fast mirror on a
# workstation and from the public registry on CI, and neither environment has
# to be able to reach the other's registry.
#
# The alternative -- pointing the lockfile at one registry forever -- makes
# every clone depend on that one host.
replace-registry-host=always
